---
import { getPluginNavItem } from "../../plugin-nav";
import DemoButton from "../astro/DemoButton.astro";
import DemoExample from "../DemoExample.astro";
import Kbd from "../astro/Kbd.astro";
import DemoSection from "../DemoSection.astro";

const plugin = getPluginNavItem("sidebar")!;

const STATE_CODE = `<!-- Everything the package exposes is this one store. -->
<ul>
  <li>Visible: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.visible"></strong></li>
  <li>matchesBreakpoint: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.matchesBreakpoint"></strong></li>
  <li>hasOverlay: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.hasOverlay"></strong></li>
</ul>

<button @click="$store.sidebar.show()">show()</button>
<button @click="$store.sidebar.hide()">hide()</button>
<button @click="$store.sidebar.toggle()">toggle()</button>

<!-- Not a single line of keyboard handling. With closeOnEscape (the default)
     the plugin installs its own keydown listener on document when it mounts, so
     Escape closes from anywhere on the page.

     And careful: do not write $store.sidebar.visible = true. In Alpine's store,
     visible is a mirrored field that sync() overwrites; only
     show()/hide()/toggle() reach the controller. -->`;

const DRAWER_CODE = `<!-- The overlay is not a second boolean: it is derived.
     hasOverlay = visible && closeOnOverlayClick, so setting
     closeOnOverlayClick:false turns it off for good, even with the
     drawer open on top of the content. -->
<div x-show="$store.sidebar.hasOverlay" x-cloak
     class="fixed inset-0 bg-black/40"
     @click="$store.sidebar.hide()"></div>

<button @click="$store.sidebar.toggle()"
        :aria-expanded="$store.sidebar.isVisible"
        aria-controls="demo-drawer">Menu</button>

<aside id="demo-drawer"
       x-show="$store.sidebar.isVisible" x-cloak
       class="fixed inset-y-0 left-0 z-10 w-64 border-r bg-[var(--ios-bg-elevated)] p-4">
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Drawer</h3>
  <!-- No @keydown and no x-trap: the package does not manage focus. Move it
       yourself on open and return it to the trigger on close. -->
  <nav>…</nav>
</aside>

<!-- The plugin is registered with whatever query suits you:
     sidebar({ breakpoint: { query: '(max-width: 1023px)', onMismatch: 'hide' } })
     onMismatch is REQUIRED inside the object: there is no default.
     And matchesBreakpoint stays false forever if you omit
     breakpoint — there is no default query. -->`;
---

<DemoSection id={plugin.id} title={plugin.title} api={plugin.api} description={plugin.description}>
	<p class="text-sm text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)] text-pretty">
		The smallest package in the Features layer: one boolean, one media query and
		one key. The drawer, its transition and its focus trap are still your
		markup — what this saves you is the state and the <code>Escape</code>.
	</p>

	<!--
		This whole page only works below 1024px, and the reason is the package's own
		registration: `breakpoint: { query: '(max-width: 1023px)', onMismatch: 'hide' }`.
		At desktop width `matchesBreakpoint` is false forever, `onMismatch: 'hide'`
		has already run, and the two examples below show a drawer pinned under a
		desktop sidebar that will never be toggled — a reader resizes nothing and
		concludes the package is broken.

		So the gate is `$store.media.breakpoint !== 'desktop'`, which is the
		playground's own `media` intervals (mobile 0 / tablet 768 / desktop 1024)
		and not a Tailwind name. It is read from `media` rather than from
		`sidebar.matchesBreakpoint` on purpose: `matchesBreakpoint` is what the
		package reports about ITS query, and gating on it would make the gate
		disappear in exactly the case the reader needs to be told about.
	-->
	<div
		x-show="$store.media.breakpoint !== 'desktop'"
		x-cloak
		data-testid="sidebar-small-screen-only"
	>
		<DemoExample
			title="Escape closes without a single @keydown in your markup"
			note="Reach for it as soon as 'open or closed?' stops being a trivial state: the store also ships hasOverlay, so the overlay is not a second boolean that drifts out of sync."
			open
			code={STATE_CODE}
		>
		<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-sm" data-testid="sidebar-state">
			<li>Visible: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.visible"></strong></li>
			<li>matchesBreakpoint: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.matchesBreakpoint"></strong></li>
			<li>hasOverlay: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.hasOverlay"></strong></li>
			<li>Breakpoint (<code>@ailura/alpinejs-media</code>): <strong x-text="$store.media.breakpoint"></strong></li>
		</ul>

		<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-3">
			<DemoButton @click="$store.sidebar.show()">show()</DemoButton>
			<DemoButton @click="$store.sidebar.hide()">hide()</DemoButton>
			<DemoButton @click="$store.sidebar.toggle()">toggle()</DemoButton>
		</div>

		<p class="text-sm text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)] mt-3">
			Open the playground drawer and press <Kbd>Esc</Kbd>: it closes through the listener
			the plugin installs on <code>document</code> when it mounts. The playground's
			<code>breakpoint</code> is <code>(max-width: 1023px)</code> with
			<code>onMismatch: 'hide'</code>, so going from mobile to desktop closes the drawer
			on its own. The <code>Breakpoint</code> line above comes from
			<code>@ailura/alpinejs-media</code>, a different package — this one does not use it.
		</p>
		</DemoExample>

		<DemoExample
			title="The drawer and its overlay: hasOverlay is derived state"
			note="No computed aria-*, no focus trap, no scroll lock. All of that is markup — but aria-expanded comes free if you read it from isVisible."
			code={DRAWER_CODE}
		>
		<div>
			<DemoButton @click="$store.sidebar.toggle()" :aria-expanded="$store.sidebar.isVisible" aria-controls="demo-drawer">
				Toggle the demo drawer
			</DemoButton>
			<span class="ml-2 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)]">
				hasOverlay: <strong x-text="$store.sidebar.hasOverlay"></strong> · visible:
				<strong x-text="$store.sidebar.isVisible"></strong>
			</span>
		</div>

		<div
			x-show="$store.sidebar.hasOverlay" x-cloak
			class="fixed inset-0 bg-black/40"
			@click="$store.sidebar.hide()"
		></div>

		<aside
			id="demo-drawer"
			x-show="$store.sidebar.isVisible" x-cloak
			class="fixed inset-y-0 left-0 z-10 w-64 overflow-auto border-r bg-[var(--ios-bg-elevated)] p-4 shadow-2xl"
		>
			<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
				<h3 class="font-semibold">Demo drawer</h3>
				<DemoButton size="sm" variant="ghost" @click="$store.sidebar.hide()">Close</DemoButton>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)]">
				<p class="rounded-md bg-[var(--ios-fill-quaternary)] px-2 py-1.5">Overview</p>
				<p class="rounded-md px-2 py-1.5">Components</p>
				<p class="rounded-md px-2 py-1.5">Patterns</p>
			</div>
			<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-tertiary)]">
				This drawer IS the playground's <code>$store.sidebar</code> — not a copy. Press
				<Kbd>Esc</Kbd>, click the overlay, or use the buttons.
			</p>
		</aside>
		</DemoExample>
	</div>

	<!--
		The desktop half. `x-show` rather than `hidden` so the two branches are
		mutually exclusive in the DOM rather than merely in paint: a reader with
		the window at 1200px sees the explanation, and one at 600px sees the
		demos, with neither able to scroll the other into view by accident.
	-->
	<div
		x-show="$store.media.breakpoint === 'desktop'"
		x-cloak
		data-testid="sidebar-needs-small-screen"
		class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-[var(--ios-fill-quaternary)] p-4"
	>
		<h4 class="text-sm font-medium">This demo needs a small screen</h4>
		<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)] text-pretty">
			The playground registers the package with
			<code>breakpoint: &#123; query: '(max-width: 1023px)', onMismatch: 'hide' &#125;</code>,
			and right now <strong x-text="$store.media.width"></strong>px wide
			(<code x-text="$store.media.breakpoint"></code>) does not satisfy that query. That is why
			<code>$store.sidebar.matchesBreakpoint</code> is <code>false</code> and
			<code>onMismatch: 'hide'</code> has already closed the drawer: there is nothing to open here.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)] text-pretty">
			Narrow the window below 1024px — or use the DevTools device toolbar with a
			phone in landscape — and the two examples above appear on their own, with no
			reload. The breakpoint is read from <code>@ailura/alpinejs-media</code>, which this
			registration defines as <code>mobile</code>/<code>tablet</code>/<code>desktop</code>:
			those are its names, not Tailwind's.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
			<DemoButton size="sm" @click="$store.media.refresh()">refresh()</DemoButton>
			<span class="text-xs text-[var(--ios-label-secondary)]">
				Forces an immediate re-read of <code>matchMedia</code> without waiting for
				<code>resize</code>.
			</span>
		</div>
	</div>
</DemoSection>
